Barnaby was not your average house cat. While his siblings were content to nap in sunbeams and chase the elusive red dot of a laser pointer, Barnaby’s emerald eyes were always fixed on the window, watching the world beyond the glass with a longing that felt too large for his cozy living room. He was a ginger tabby with a white chest and a spirit that felt too large for his cozy living room. He dreamed of the Great Beyond, the mysterious land beyond the garden fence that his mother had warned him about with a stern flick of her tail.

One crisp autumn evening, the back door was left slightly ajar. To Barnaby, it wasn’t an accident; it was an invitation. He slipped through the gap, his paws silent on the cool wood, and stepped into the garden. Under the moonlight, the familiar yard transformed into a vast wilderness. The rose bushes became jagged cliffs, the garden hose was a sleeping serpent, and the shadows stretched long and thin like ghostly fingers.

